Unit 3: User Interface design
=============================

3.1 Graphical and web user interfaces
=====================================
Graphical User Interface (GUI)
==============================
A Graphical User Interface (GUI) is a type of interface where users interact with a system using
graphical elements instead of typing commands.

GUI uses:
========
Windows
Buttons
Icons
Menus
Dialog boxes
Toolbars

Users interact mainly through:
==============================
Mouse
Touch
Keyboard

Examples of GUI Applications
============================
Microsoft Word
Adobe Photoshop
File Explorer

Features of GUI
===============
Feature	                      Description
Windows	                      Separate work areas
Icons	                        Small graphical symbols
Menus	                        List of commands
Buttons	                      Trigger actions
Drag and Drop	                Move items visually

Web User Interface (Web UI)
===========================

A Web UI is the interface of a website or web application accessed through a 
browser.

Users interact through:
=======================
Web pages
Forms
Navigation bars
Cards
Buttons
Responsive layouts

Examples of Web UI
==================
Google Docs
YouTube
Facebook
Figma

GUI vs Web UI
============
Feature	                  GUI	                        Web UI
=======                   ====                        ======
Platform	                Desktop OS	                Web Browser
Internet Needed	          Usually No	                Usually Yes
Installation	            Required	                  Usually Not
Speed	                    Faster for heavy apps	      Depends on browser/network
Technologies	            Java, C#, Qt	              HTML, CSS, JS
Examples	                Photoshop, VLC	            Gmail, Figma

3.2 Interaction styles: Command line, Menu selection, Form fill in, Direct 
manipulation, Antrhorpomorphic

Command Line Interface (CLI)
============================
A Command Line Interface (CLI) is a text-based user interface where users interact
with a computer by typing commands instead of clicking buttons or icons. It is one 
of the oldest types of user interfaces.

Menu selection
==============
A Menu Selection Interface is a type of user interface where users choose options 
from a displayed list or menu instead of typing commands.

It is commonly used in:
======================
Desktop applications
Websites
Mobile apps
ATMs
Smart TVs
Embedded systems

Form Fill-In Interface
======================
A Form Fill-In Interface is a type of user interface where users enter information
into fields, boxes, checklists, or dropdown menus.

It is widely used in:
=====================
Registration forms
Login pages
Online applications
Surveys
Payment systems
Desktop software forms

Direct manipulation
===================
A Direct Manipulation Interface is a type of user interface where users interact 
directly with visible objects on the screen using actions like:

Dragging
Dropping
Resizing
Rotating
Swiping
Zooming

Instead of typing commands, users manipulate graphical objects directly.

Antrhorpomorphic
================
An Anthropomorphic Interface is a user interface designed to behave or appear like 
a human.

The system may:

Speak like a person
Show emotions
Use facial expressions
Act like a virtual assistant
Mimic human conversation

The word anthropomorphic is “giving human characteristics to non-human systems.”


3.3. Principles of UI design
============================
Principles of UI Design
=======================
1. Clarity
==========
The interface should be easy to understand.
Buttons, icons, and menus should clearly show their purpose.
Users should know what to do without confusion.

Example:
=======
A trash icon for delete
A search icon for search

2. Consistency
==============
Use the same styles and behaviors throughout the interface.
Same button colors
Same font styles
Same navigation patterns

Consistency helps users learn faster.

Example:
========
All “Save” buttons should look identical.

3. Simplicity
=============
Keep the design clean and avoid unnecessary elements.

Remove clutter
Use minimal text
Focus on important actions

Simple interfaces improve usability.

4. Feedback
===========
The system should respond to user actions.

Examples:
=========
Button changes color when clicked
Loading spinner appears (A loading spinner is a visual animation that tells users the system is 
processing something or waiting for data to load)
Success message after saving

Feedback tells users what is happening.

5. Visibility
=============
Important elements should be easy to find.

Menus should be visible
Important buttons should stand out
Information hierarchy should be clear

Example:
========
“Login” button highlighted prominently

6. Accessibility
================
Design should be usable by everyone, including people with disabilities.

Examples:
=========
Large readable text
Good color contrast
Keyboard navigation
Screen reader support

Accessibility improves usability for all users.

7. User Control
===============
Users should feel in control of the interface.

Examples:
=========
Undo button
Back button
Cancel option

Avoid forcing users into unwanted actions.

8. Efficiency
=============
Tasks should be completed quickly and easily.

Examples:
=========
Short navigation paths
Keyboard shortcuts
Auto-complete search

Efficient interfaces save time.

9. Visual Hierarchy
===================
Arrange elements according to importance.

Methods:
========
Larger text for headings
Bold important buttons
Use spacing and colors wisely

This guides user attention.

10. Error Prevention and Recovery
==================================
Help users avoid mistakes and recover easily.

Examples:
=========
Confirmation before deleting
Form validation (Form validation is the process of checking whether the data entered into a form 
is correct, complete, and acceptable before submission, it helps prevent errors and improves user
experience)
Helpful error messages

Good UI reduces frustration.

3.4 Graphical user interface
============================
Same as 3.1


3.5 UI design process
=====================
The UI (User Interface) design process is a structured set of steps used to design
visually effective, user-friendly interfaces for websites, apps, and software.

It focuses on how the product looks and how users interact with it.

1. Research (Understanding Users)
=================================
This is the first step where designers understand:
User needs
Target audience
Problems to solve
Competitor apps

Common activities:
User interviews
Surveys
Market research

2. Define Requirements
======================
After research, designers define:
What features are needed
What screens are required
User goals
Business goals

Example:
========
Login screen
Dashboard
Profile page

3. Wireframing
==============
Wireframes are basic layout sketches of screens.

They show:
==========
Structure
Placement of elements
Navigation flow

No colors or graphics yet.


4. UI Design (Visual Design)
============================
This step adds:
Colors
Typography
Icons
Images
Branding

Tools used:
==========
Figma
Adobe XD

5. Prototyping
==============
A prototype is a clickable model of the UI.

It allows users to:
Navigate screens
Test interactions
Simulate real app behavior

6. User Testing
===============
Real users test the design to check:
Usability
Confusing elements
Navigation issues
Overall experience

Feedback is collected to improve design.

7. Iteration (Improvement)
=========================
Based on feedback:
Fix design problems
Improve layout
Enhance usability
Update interactions

This step may repeat multiple times.

8. Handoff to Developers
========================
Final UI is given to developers with:
Design files
Style guides
Component specs
Assets (icons/images)

Tools used:
===========
Figma (Dev Mode)
Zeplin
Adobe XD export tools


3.6 Human considerations in interface and screen design
=======================================================
Human considerations in UI and screen design refer to designing interfaces based 
on how real people see, think, and behave. The goal is to make systems easy, 
comfortable, safe, and efficient to use.

This comes from Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) principles.
============================================================

1. Perception (How users see things)
====================================
Design should match human visual perception:
Clear contrast between text and background
Readable font sizes
Proper spacing and alignment
Avoid visual clutter

2. Memory (Limited human memory)
===============================
Humans cannot remember too many things at once, so design should:
Avoid forcing users to memorize steps
Use menus instead of commands
Show hints, labels, and icons
Keep instructions visible

Example:
Menu selection is easier than CLI commands

3. Attention (Focus and distraction)
====================================
Users focus only on a few things at a time:
Highlight important actions (buttons, alerts)
Avoid unnecessary animations
Use visual hierarchy (titles → content → details)

4. Learning and Familiarity
===========================
Interfaces should be easy to learn:
Use familiar icons (home, search, settings)
Keep consistent layouts
Follow platform standards (Android/iOS/Web patterns)

Example:
Gear icon = Settings (universally understood)

5. Motor Skills (Physical interaction)
======================================
Design should consider how users interact physically:
Large clickable buttons
Easy touch targets (especially on mobile)
Avoid tiny clickable elements
Support keyboard and mouse navigation

6. Error Prevention and Recovery
===============================
Humans make mistakes, so interfaces should:
Prevent errors (validation, confirmations)
Show clear error messages
Allow undo/redo options

Example:
“Are you sure you want to delete this file?”

7. Feedback (System response)
=============================
Users need to know what is happening:
Loading spinners
Success messages
Button animations
Progress bars

8. Consistency
==============
Users learn faster when designs are consistent:
Same button styles across screens
Same color meanings (red = error, green = success)
Predictable navigation

9. Accessibility (Inclusive design)
===================================
Design must support all users:
Color-blind friendly palettes
Screen reader support
Keyboard navigation
High contrast mode

10. Cognitive Load (Mental effort)
==================================
Reduce user thinking effort:
Simple layouts
Clear labels
Group related items
Avoid too many choices


3.7 Technological considerations in interface design
====================================================
Technological considerations refer to the hardware, software, and system limitations
or capabilities that influence how a user interface (UI) is designed and implemented.
In simple terms, UI must not only be good for humans, but also feasible for 
technology.

1. Device and Platform Constraints
==================================
UI must adapt to different devices:
Desktop computers
Mobile phones
Tablets
Smart TVs
Wearables

Each has different:
Screen sizes
Input methods (mouse, touch, voice)
Processing power

2. Screen Resolution and Display Quality
========================================
Design must consider:
High-resolution (HD, 4K) vs low-resolution screens
Pixel density (retina displays)
Scaling of UI elements

Key idea:
UI must remain clear and readable on all screen sizes

3. Performance and Speed
========================
UI should be optimized for system performance:
Fast loading time
Smooth animations
Minimal lag
Efficient resource usage (CPU, RAM)

Example:
Heavy animations may slow low-end devices

4. Network Connectivity
=======================
For web and cloud applications:
Internet speed affects UI experience
Offline support may be required
Data should load progressively

Example:
========
Loading skeleton screens in apps

5. Input Technology
===================
Different systems support different inputs:
Keyboard and mouse (desktop)
Touch gestures (mobile)
Voice input (VUI)
Stylus input (tablets)
Motion sensors (AR/VR)

Design must match input method.

6. Browser and Software Compatibility
=====================================
For web UI:
Works across browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari)
Must follow web standards (HTML, CSS, JavaScript)
Responsive design is required

Example:
A website should not break in different browsers

7. Scalability
==============
UI should handle:
Small number of users → large number of users
Small data → large datasets

Example:
Dashboards showing thousands of records efficiently

8. Security Considerations
==========================
UI must support secure interactions:
Login authentication
Encryption of sensitive data
Secure forms (banking, payments)
Protection from attacks (e.g., XSS)

9. Integration with Backend Systems
===================================
UI depends on backend services:
APIs (data fetching)
Databases
Cloud services

Example:
========
A login form connects to authentication server

10. Update and Maintenance
==========================
UI should be easy to:
Update without breaking features
Deploy quickly (especially web apps)
Maintain across versions

Example:
Web apps update instantly without reinstalling

11. Accessibility Technology Support
====================================
UI must support assistive technologies:
Screen readers
Keyboard navigation
Voice control systems
High contrast modes
















